WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.367
Anderson.

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:01.501
24 hours after the

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.902
polls closed on the West Coast,

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.170
we still have, as we did

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:04.637
two years

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:05.605
ago, uncertainty

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.173
about two very important states

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.709
in American politics, including Arizona.

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:10.543
And Nevada.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:11.878
So you just heard from Gary

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:12.445
and from Kyung,

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:14.481
let's start in Arizona, where Kyung is,

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.148
as she noted,

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.483
the last time votes were reported,

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:17.851
Mark Kelly's lead increase.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:18.752
The key is to watch

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:19.719
the next batch of votes,

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:20.887
the one after that,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.922
if you remember the dynamics in 20, 20.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:23.890
It was Joe Biden's lead

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:24.858
that kept shrinking.

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:25.892
He won the state of the end,

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:26.993
but his lead was shrinking

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:27.927
as the ballots come in.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:28.695
So the key is

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:29.963
what happens as we go forward.

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:31.531
And David laid out the percentages.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:32.098
In Nevada,

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.333
we have the percentages for

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:34.267
Arizona as well.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.802
So we have to watch this play out.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.670
A vital state, obviously a vital race.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:38.705
A Democratic incumbent

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.707
facing a challenger from a Trump

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:41.875
endorsed Republican.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.643
They're critical to the 50

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:44.944
ballot 50 50 balance

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.112
we currently have in the Senate.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:46.980
Where will it end up?

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.648
And then quickly in Nevada

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:49.783
this is where Gary is.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:50.950
He's in Clark County.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.052
It's 75%.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.920
74% of the state vote.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.021
And the question is,

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:55.855
can Cortez Masto

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:56.856
improve that percentage

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.058
as more and more votes come in?

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.759
That's what she needs to overcome.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:00.693
One other key question

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.662
there is can she turn Washoe?

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:03.363
Washoe County

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:04.464
here, where Reno is,

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:06.466
and then the rural areas to the north.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.201
That's a swing county,

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:09.702
usually the color of this county in

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.538
the end can determine who wins the state.

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:12.839
Laxalt with a slight

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:13.773
lead there right now.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:14.941
Again, more votes to be counted.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:16.276
I just want to come to this other map

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:17.510
just very quickly

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.812
to get to the question here,

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:20.346
which is why do these races matter?

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:20.780
Well, of course,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:21.181
they matter

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.283
to the citizens of Arizona and Nevada.

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.150
They also matter to

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.585
the dynamics here in Washington.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.587
Who controls the United States Senate?

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.922
This is a runoff in Georgia.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.490
We know that now on December six.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.658
The question is

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:34.127
if Senator Kelly can keep his lead here

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:38.932
and Senator Laxalt can keep

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:39.933
that lead here.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.034
Neither of these are guaranteed,

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:41.935
by the way, as you heard

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:42.735
both correspondents,

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:43.803
tens of thousands of votes

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:44.971
still to be counted,

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.039
but there's no consequential,

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.073
because if that happened,

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:49.275
if this those two races stayed the same,

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:49.976
then guess what?

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.312
Republicans have 50 Democrats at 49.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.213
And once again,

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:53.646
the Georgia

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.381
runoff would determine the control

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.316
the United States Senate

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.784
if Democrats would get back to 50

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:58.885
where they are now.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.386
The vice president breaks the tie.

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.921
Republicans get to 51.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.424
So any change in those races affects

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.059
whether or not this Georgia

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:08.161
runoff in December is decisive Anderson.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.162
And we're going to have to wait

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.663
listening to Kyung and Gary

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.731
for a while to get that.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:12.365
And just quickly,

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:13.099
I just want to come over.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.367
You mentioned the House totals

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.135
at the top of the race.

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.069
I just want to show you the map

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.004
is it fills it looks like a lot of red,

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.072
but Republicans are not

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:20.106
at the finish line.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:21.541
Republicans thought by now,

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:23.309
they thought by this time last night,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.043
we would be saying

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.578
there will be a Republican majority

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:26.613
in the House of Representatives.

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.214
Here we are Wednesday night.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.216
11 07 in the East.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:32.252
Republicans are called there.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:33.820
We have call 209.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.788
So they are now nine House seats

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.390
short of 218.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.526
The magic number for majority Democrats.

00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:41.895
20 seats behind them right now at one 89.

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:43.229
Let's look where they're ahead.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.097
This is really interesting

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.932
Republicans are ahead right now

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:46.966
in enough seats

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:47.467
enough seats

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.269
if nothing change to get a majority.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:50.737
But look how narrow a majority

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:52.739
it would be if it stopped right now

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:53.973
it's not going to stop right now.

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:55.408
Some of these races will take days.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.943
Some could take longer than that

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:57.810
to count.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.412
But you just get the expectation

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.680
from looking at those numbers

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.048
where they stand right now

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.217
that, yes, the probability is Republicans

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.318
get to a majority.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:06.085
It's also likely

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.787
that's going to be a very narrow

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:08.288
majority.

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:09.222
Well, one last point,

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:10.523
Anderson, before I come back.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:10.890
Not enough

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.192
attention, in my view, has been paid

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:13.026
to this

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.360
Democratic governor

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.795
gets a new Democratic governor

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:16.563
in Pennsylvania,

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:17.797
Democratic governor reelected

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:18.498
in Michigan,

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:19.299
Democratic governor

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.700
reelected in Wisconsin,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.001
Democratic governor

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.670
reelected in Minnesota.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.138
We talk a lot about the battleground

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:25.872
states out here.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:26.639
They matter

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.107
who will win the governorship in Nevada,

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:29.943
who will win the governorship in Arizona.

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:31.277
These states right here.

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:31.978
A long time ago,

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:33.313
we used to call it the blue wall

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:34.514
they're incredibly important.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:35.248
That was a little messy.

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:36.216
Let me do it one more time.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:37.951
You come up here right in there. Yes.

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.652
The governor of Ohio was reelected,

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.187
but the Democrats held

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.423
Pennsylvania, held Michigan and held

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.558
Wisconsin in these governor's races

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:46.059
and under,

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.527
I think, underappreciated story

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.461
for the Democratic Party

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:49.395
in these midterm results.

